so i have a broad question to start with  because i might have seen your comment  on  my clip from my interview with marcia  about confident actors  so i wanted to take that and pose it to  you in a different way i know confidence  top to bottom is near impossible in this  industry but  what would you say is the key to  maintaining even like a little degree of  it to  forge forward with positivity about your  work  oh man it's the hardest thing to do  i i have struggled more with confidence  in my work  than i ever have in my life  i guess part of the key i have taken  great solace  in in working with actors who are  better than me who have been around for  a lot longer than me  and hearing from them that it never gets  easier  i know that sounds dark but something  about knowing that you're not alone in  that experience  gives you the the the confidence to at  least keep going i suppose  well you're definitely surrounding  yourself with some good ones because  marsha is hands down one of my favorites  so  what is something about what marsha does  for you on set  as a scene partner that you wish more  actors brought to set  she's honest in her work her range is  incredible  the way that she can take a breath and  sink in and open her eyes and completely  transform in front of your eyes  is so awe inspiring but marcia stephanie  is also the kind of scene partner and  the kind of friend  who will tell you when something sucks  and there is  nothing more valuable than having people  like that in your corner she will tell  you kindly  but she will tell you to take another  crack at it and i and i  i wish more people were able to kindly  let you know when you should give it  another whack  there's a real art to be able to do that  the right way and it makes me even more  impressed by her work  so from one seat partner to another here  i did want to ask you about working with  a baby so much on this movie  my first question actually is was there  ever a fake baby onset or was it always  a real infant  so rarely i have never been a part of  something  where that that involved children and  babies where we used the fake babies so  little  like it was incredibly ambitious but  julia and i spoke a lot about  particularly julia how important  having the baby present was for this  film  because while we talk so much about this  being a journey that  gene undergoes um it's really a it's a  partnership it's a developing of their  relationship and it's a journey that  they largely undergo together  so being able to see the baby's face and  being able to watch the baby grow  we actually shot primarily in  chronological order  so you literally get to watch this baby  grow up before your eyes  i mean it was extraordinary but holy  [ __ ] was it hard  what is an example of a time on set when  working with a real  infant forced you to adapt or change  something as you were going through a  scene  it happened every single day but the the  biggest example because we were  concerned about what the story  implications of it would be  is that there is this there's a scene  with with jean and cow in the diner  where jean  sort of spills her guts to cow and  finally lets  him in and lets him know who she is and  one of the things she talks about is her  lack of confidence  in her relationship with harry but  mentions that she sings to him  and there was a scene in the film before  that scene  where you saw gene singing terry and you  saw how capable she was and how  close they were before she realizes it  and we felt that that was important for  the audience to get to see it and  understand that even though they can and  everyone else can that gene can't see it  herself  but that day the babies were not having  it we tried over the course of  a couple of different days to get that  scene and  and they just were not into my singing  um  and we ended up cutting the scene  but then and this is another gift of  working with babies  there was a day much later in the film  we were shooting in the cabin  it was a late night the babies were kind  of tired but in a good mood  and we were messing around and i just i  started  humming natural woman and our and our dp  picked up the camera and pointed it at  us  and the baby was i forget which one we  were working with that day probably  justin but the baby was sort of  reaching for me and interacting with me  in a way that we could only have hoped  for then  and somehow later in the film felt even  more powerful  it kept it they kept us on our toes  though for sure what a cool thing to  have had come up like that because  that's such a powerful moment  yeah so also this is a first feature for  you as a producer  and that's a pretty huge accomplishment  even with all of your experience on sets  thus far  is there anything about what it takes to  produce a feature that kind of caught  you off guard or surprised you  the length of time it takes um  which was also by the same token a huge  gift and one of the unexpected pieces of  moving into this role as a producer in  addition to being an actor  but i first read the script in and it  can take much much longer than this but  i first read the skip  script in 2018 and we  were still in the sort of late stages of  script development  developing the relationship with amazon  and and um  and sort of asking them to support this  film and the way that we felt like it  would like it deserved and  and of course given the relationship  that i had with them we knew we we knew  that they would  um but also you know all the all the way  through pre-production all the way  through post-production i had never  been through a full post-production  process on a film before  and it is it's now i understand what  goes on in all that time when as an  actor you're waiting for this movie to  come out going my god what's taking so  long  now i know very intimately uh but it was  also really exciting to be able to spend  especially on the front end of the  project  that much intimate time with the  character and to have that additional  context  for the you know from the script  development process and also to be able  to have  the other producer the writers and the  director  in constant conversation regarding the  project and the role  made me a better actor too all the time  that went into it is well worth it it's  fantastic before they kick me out of  here  i want to ask you about the golden arm  because i'm a big samurai i'm a big sam  rainey fan  i know what happened with the viral clip  but i'm curious from your perspective  with that clip that goes out there  it almost the whole episode overall  almost felt like a little bit of an  in-joke in a very classic sam raimi  style and that clip  goes viral without the ending so do you  feel like it was kind of a situation of  people  i don't know getting the joke but not  getting the punch line  i don't think they got the joke like it  was i think that was the most  frustrating part about it because  you would know as a fan of sam raimi i  mean the huge reason to do this project  i had this meeting with sam raimi having  been familiar with his work from afar  you fall in love with the man instantly  the passion that he has for this  practical horror which is not  necessarily a genre that i would have  been attracted to  is infectious it's so exciting i mean it  and it was like that on set it was one  of the most fun experiences i've ever  had  and you you know you have this  experience of someone like sam raimi  going more blood more blood cannons let  me pour the blood all over you  and and you know you know it's camp it's  it's classic  sam raimi camp and so it was kind of a  bummer for a project that i think very  much  was what it set out to be for this more  serious part of the the the show  to go viral so that people could rip it  out of that context that truthfully  can bring people so much joy  well well while it was short-lived it  did bring me some joy so thank you for  that  huge congratulations on this i can't  wait for everyone to see it
 

